What it does

Most weather apps tell you there's a chance of storms. Strikewise tells you where lightning is striking right now, how close it is to you, and when it's safe again.

It plots live cloud-to-ground strikes on a map around your location, layers them over live weather radar, pulls in official National Weather Service alerts, and continuously answers the one question that actually matters when there's thunder outside: should I be inside right now?

Built over four months, from a first MVP to a live system that has caught multiple real NWS-warned severe thunderstorms.


Features

🗺️ Live strike map

Real lightning strikes plotted in real time and color-aged as they fade — so you can see at a glance where a storm is now versus where it's been. Tap any strike to inspect its distance, age, polarity, and thunder ETA. Tap anywhere on the map to move your monitored point.

🛡️ Safety radius

Three configurable rings around your location — Caution (25 km) · Warning (16 km) · Danger (8 km). The app tracks the closest strike against those rings and shifts its status from SAFE → CAUTION → WARNING → DANGER, complete with a plain-English recommendation ("Dangerous lightning activity is close. Go indoors now.").

⏱️ Thunder ETA

A live countdown to when you'll hear the thunder from a given strike, computed from the strike's distance and the speed of sound (343 m/s), adjusted for how long ago the strike happened. The oldest storm instinct there is — "one-Mississippi" — turned into the interface.

⏳ 30-minute all-clear timer

Follows the real NWS lightning-safety guideline: wait 30 minutes after the last nearby strike before heading back out. The app runs the countdown so you don't have to guess.

🌧️ Live weather radar

NOAA/NWS NEXRAD radar rendered underneath the strikes, so lightning is shown in the context of the actual storm producing it.

🚨 Official NWS alerts

Severe Thunderstorm Watches and Warnings pulled directly from the National Weather Service and surfaced in-app the moment they're issued for your area.

📊 Live strike telemetry

Strike rate per minute with a rising/falling trend, closest strike, latest strike, 10-minute totals, and a running feed of recent cloud-to-ground strikes as they land.

🔀 Multi-provider failover

The backend abstracts five different lightning data sources (Blitzortung, NOAA GOES GLM, Xweather, Tomorrow.io, Open-Meteo) behind a single provider registry with automatic failover. If one feed goes dark, the app keeps working — a resilience most weather apps don't have.

🤖 ML storm nowcasting

A Python service clusters incoming strikes with DBSCAN, fits their centroid movement with linear regression, and projects where the storm is headed ~15 minutes out — drawn on the map as a predicted track.

📱 Focus / Full mode

A toggle that lets you decide how much detail you want: Focus for a stripped-down "am I safe?" glance, Full for every panel and readout.

🌎 Works anywhere

No per-city setup. Location-aware and built on nationwide data sources — tested live in both Michigan and Atlanta.


